\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Abelian directions of exponent at least 3}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{N\ge 3} and let #{G=(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^n}. The forward
labels are the #{n} standard generators, and the symmetric labels
are those generators together with their inverses. Both sets are
conjugation-stable. The unit-weight derived form pairs distinct
labels by zero and gives each label square #{1}. Those values leave
the group law untwisted, so the direction algebra is the commutative
group algebra #{k[G]}, of rank #{N^n}. A Clifford algebra of the
direction module has a different rank, except for the symmetric
calculus of exponent #{4}, where the multiplications disagree. The
generators commute and have order #{N}, so the root of unity in a
generalized Clifford shape is #{1}.}

\proof{
\p{An abelian group has a trivial conjugation action, so every set
of labels is conjugation-stable. The trivial cocycle
#{F=1} satisfies the identity of \ref{fdip-00QG}, and the resulting
product is the group law. A Clifford algebra of a free module of
rank #{r} has rank #{2^r}, by the symbol isomorphism of
\ref{fdip-00QB} applied to the diagonal form of square #{1} on each
label. The forward module has rank #{n}, and the symmetric module
has rank #{2n}. The equality #{N^n=2^n} forces #{N=2}, and
#{N^n=4^n} forces #{N=4}. Thus the only rank collision with
#{N\ge 3} is the symmetric calculus of exponent #{4}.}

\p{On that calculus let #{t} generate one cyclic factor, and let
#{e,f} be two distinct direction labels of the fibre. In the
Clifford algebra #{ef=-fe}. If #{2\neq 0} in #{k}, then
#{ef\neq fe}, while #{k[G]} is commutative. If #{2=0}, the labels
commute and square to #{1}. Translate each label #{e} by #{1}.
The translate squares to #{0}. A polynomial in commuting
translates of square #{0} has square equal to the square of its
constant term, by the freshman's dream. Thus every square in the
Clifford algebra lies in #{k\cdot 1}. But #{(t+1)^2=t^2+1} is not
a scalar on the free basis of #{k[G]}. The algebras are not
isomorphic.}
}
